"""Original discussion outlines, separate from paraphrased source prompts."""

def outline(title, topic):
    t=title.lower()
    rules=[
      (['bias-variance','overfitting','underfitting'], 'Compare training and validation errors to distinguish high bias from high variance. Use a held-out or appropriately grouped/time-split test set. For high bias, consider stronger features or model capacity; for high variance, consider regularisation, more representative data or reduced capacity. Report the target metric and explain any distribution shift.'),
      (['bagging','boosting','random forest','dt vs rf'], 'Bagging trains models on resampled data and aggregates them, typically reducing variance. Random forests add feature subsampling. Boosting adds learners sequentially to improve an objective or focus on residual errors. Compare accuracy, calibration, training cost and sensitivity to noise rather than claiming one method always wins.'),
      (['xgboost'], 'Explain gradient boosting as additive optimisation using loss gradients and, in XGBoost, second-order information. Discuss depth, learning rate, subsampling, regularisation and early stopping. Parallelism can accelerate split statistics within a boosting step; boosting rounds themselves still depend on earlier rounds.'),
      (['calibration'], 'A calibrated probability of 0.7 should correspond to about 70% positives within that prediction group. Evaluate reliability curves and probabilistic scores such as Brier score or log loss. Fit calibration only on data independent of model training, and check calibration by segment and after distribution shift.'),
      (['svm'], 'Distinguish linear and kernel SVMs. Complexity depends on solver, number of examples, feature dimension, sparsity and kernel; there is no single universally correct training complexity. Explain the margin objective, C, kernel choice and why large datasets often favour linear or approximate methods.'),
      (['cross-entropy','kl divergence','kl term','kl-regular','kl penalty'], 'For discrete distributions, H(p,q) = −Σ p(x) log q(x), and KL(p||q) = Σ p(x) log[p(x)/q(x)] = H(p,q) − H(p). KL is asymmetric and becomes infinite if q assigns zero probability where p is positive. Explain the direction of the KL term and its coefficient before discussing a training objective.'),
      (['data leakage','missing data'], 'Look for future information, target-derived features, duplicate entities across splits and transformations fitted before splitting. Fit preprocessing on training data only. Analyse missingness, use an explicit baseline and evaluate on a split matching deployment. Prefer a simple valid experiment over an elaborate leaky one.'),
      (['regularisation'], 'Contrast L1 sparsity, L2 weight shrinkage, dropout, early stopping and augmentation. Increasing regularisation can reduce variance while increasing bias. Choose strength on validation data, inspect learning curves, and keep preprocessing and model selection separate from the final test set.'),
      (['precision','recall','roc-auc','metrics'], 'Precision = TP/(TP+FP); recall = TP/(TP+FN). Define the positive class and zero-denominator convention. Choose metrics based on error costs and prevalence; PR curves are useful for rare positives. Threshold selection is a decision problem, while ROC-AUC measures ranking across thresholds.'),
      (['weighted','multinomial'], 'Build cumulative nonnegative weights. Draw a uniform value in [0, total_weight) and binary-search the first cumulative sum greater than the draw. Handle zero weights and reject a zero total. Preprocessing is O(n), sampling O(log n). Discuss an alias table for many repeated samples and test empirical frequencies.'),
      (['uniform 0','biased coin','random bits'], 'Use a von Neumann extractor for independent biased coin flips: HT and TH have equal probability, while HH and TT are discarded. Build unbiased bits, then use rejection sampling to obtain an integer in 0…6 from three bits, rejecting 7. Independence and a nondegenerate bias are required.'),
      (['circle'], 'For a uniform point in a disk of radius R, sample angle θ uniformly in [0,2π) and radius r = R√U with U uniform in [0,1). Sampling r uniformly overweights the centre. Return (r cos θ, r sin θ). Check radial CDF P(r≤a)=a²/R².'),
      (['population mean','conversion rate','two user','two populations','p-value','p-tests','t-tests','completion rate'], 'State the estimand, unit of analysis, hypothesis and sampling assumptions. Use confidence intervals and an appropriate test for the outcome, checking independence and clustering. Separate statistical significance from effect size and practical impact. Account for repeated peeking, multiple comparisons and selection bias.'),
      (['propensity','causal','causality','causal impact'], 'Specify treatment, outcome and confounders before choosing an estimator. Randomisation is preferred when feasible. Observational methods require assumptions such as overlap and no unmeasured confounding; propensity scores alone do not establish causality. Check balance and perform sensitivity analysis.'),
      (['mixed-effects'], 'Use fixed effects for shared population relationships and random effects for clustered units such as users, stores or regions. Explain within-cluster dependence, partial pooling and assumptions about random effects. Compare to cluster-robust inference or separate models using the actual data structure.'),
      (['bandit'], 'Contrast exploration with exploitation and define reward, horizon and feedback delay. Describe epsilon-greedy, UCB or Thompson sampling, then discuss nonstationarity and delayed or biased feedback. Offline evaluation requires logged propensities and careful support assumptions.'),
      (['vae','elbo','reparameterisation'], 'Start from log p(x) and introduce q(z|x). The ELBO is E_q[log p(x|z)] − KL(q(z|x)||p(z)). For a diagonal Gaussian, use z = μ + σ⊙ε with ε∼N(0,I) to backpropagate through sampling. Distinguish a lower bound from the exact likelihood and discuss posterior collapse.'),
      (['layernorm','layernorm','batchnorm',' bn '], 'BatchNorm uses statistics across a batch; LayerNorm normalises a token’s feature dimension independently of other examples. Explain training/inference behaviour, small batches and variable sequence lengths. Discuss pre-norm versus post-norm residual layouts instead of assuming every transformer has the same placement.'),
      (['adam','optimiser','optimizer'], 'Derive the gradient step first, then describe Adam’s moving averages of gradients and squared gradients, bias correction and epsilon. Distinguish coupled L2 regularisation from AdamW’s decoupled weight decay. Discuss learning-rate schedules, clipping and numerical precision.'),
      (['cnn','convolution','u-net','yolo','nms'], 'Explain local receptive fields, shared kernels and translation-related inductive bias. A convolution’s parameter count is kernel_h × kernel_w × input_channels × output_channels, plus bias if present. Account for stride, padding and dilation in shapes. For NMS, sort candidate boxes by score and suppress boxes with excessive IoU.'),
      (['vision','multimodal'], 'Define input modalities, supervision and downstream metrics. Track tensor shapes through encoders, projection layers and fusion. Discuss resolution/token count, frozen versus trainable encoders, data alignment, modality imbalance and failure slices. Evaluate grounding as well as aggregate accuracy.'),
      (['audio','speech','voice-activity'], 'Specify sampling rate, frame/window sizes and whether latency or offline accuracy matters. Discuss waveform versus spectrogram features, speaker-independent splits and noisy environments. For disentanglement, define what information each latent should retain and how you will test leakage between content and speaker representations.'),
      (['kv cache','kv-cache'], 'In autoregressive decoding, cache each layer’s keys and values for past tokens; only compute the new token’s projections. Distinguish prefill from decoding. Account for cache size using layers × tokens × KV heads × head dimension × two tensors × bytes per value. Discuss batching, eviction, quantisation and positional-index correctness.'),
      (['attention','sqrt(d','transformer','rope','bert','gpt','swiglu'], 'Track Q, K and V shapes, then derive softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k)V. The scaling controls score variance as head dimension grows. Explain causal/padding masks, residuals, normalisation and positional information. Standard dense attention has quadratic score memory in sequence length; fused attention avoids materialising all scores but does not make the basic arithmetic linear.'),
      (['mixture-of-experts','moe','expert parallel'], 'Separate total parameter count from parameters activated per token. Explain routing, top-k expert selection, load balancing, capacity and dropped tokens. Expert parallelism introduces all-to-all communication and uneven utilisation; fewer active parameters do not automatically imply lower serving latency.'),
      (['tokenis','tokeniz','embeddings'], 'Compare word, character, byte and subword tokenisation with vocabulary-size, sequence-length and multilingual trade-offs. Embeddings can be static or contextual. For retrieval, choose pooling, normalisation and similarity consistently, and evaluate the embedding model on the target queries rather than only generic benchmarks.'),
      (['reward hacking','grpo','ppo','dpo','sft','post-training','rl alignment'], 'Distinguish supervised imitation, preference optimisation and policy-gradient methods. Define the reward or preference data, reference policy and KL regularisation. Explain clipping/baselines or group-relative advantages where applicable. Test reward exploitation, distribution shift and whether improvements survive independent human evaluation.'),
      (['importance sampling'], 'Importance sampling reweights samples from proposal q using p/q to estimate expectations under target p. The proposal must cover the target support. Explain variance, effective sample size and weight clipping/self-normalisation, including the bias introduced by stabilisation choices.'),
      (['annotator','llm-as-judge','hallucination','quality-validation',' llm system'], 'Define a rubric and a representative evaluation set. Separate correctness, grounding, usefulness and safety. Use multiple human annotations where helpful, measure agreement, and calibrate model judges against humans. Guard against position bias, judge leakage and a test set that was reused during optimisation.'),
      (['rag','retriev','vector db','chatbot','llm-based q&a','nl q&a'], 'Separate ingestion, chunking, embedding, retrieval, reranking and generation. Evaluate retrieval recall and answer faithfulness separately. Explain metadata filtering, access control, citations, update cadence and latency/cost. Treat retrieved text as untrusted input and test prompt injection. Fine-tuning changes model behaviour; retrieval is useful for changing external knowledge.'),
      (['k-means','k pickup','k-medians'], 'K-means alternates nearest-centroid assignments with mean updates, reducing squared Euclidean distortion until convergence to a local optimum. Initialise carefully, handle empty clusters and compare restarts. K-medians with L1 distance uses coordinate-wise medians; substituting a mean changes the objective.'),
      (['logistic','linear regression','gradient descent'], 'State the objective and tensor shapes. Linear least squares has normal equations, but QR/SVD are often preferable to explicitly inverting XᵀX. Logistic regression uses sigmoid predictions and cross-entropy; its gradient is Xᵀ(p−y)/n. Check finite differences, stability and scaling before tuning the optimiser.'),
      (['autograd','backprop','matmul'], 'Draw the computation graph and apply the chain rule in reverse topological order, accumulating gradients along every incoming path. For C=AB, dA=dC Bᵀ and dB=Aᵀ dC. Sum gradients over broadcast dimensions and shared parameters. Validate small examples with finite differences.'),
      (['softmax','cross-entropy fwd'], 'Use max-subtraction and log-sum-exp for numerical stability. Derive gradients from the precise loss reduction and target representation. Include shape, overflow and empty-input tests, and compare finite-difference gradients on small tensors.'),
      (['greedy, top-k','top-p','beam'], 'Greedy chooses the highest-probability next token. Top-k truncates to k tokens; nucleus sampling retains the smallest set whose cumulative probability exceeds p, then renormalises. Apply temperature before sampling, handle EOS and masks, and use deterministic seeds for tests.'),
      (['tf-idf','bag-of-words'], 'Specify tokenisation and the exact term-frequency and inverse-document-frequency formula. Fit vocabulary and document frequencies on the corpus, normalise vectors and handle out-of-vocabulary terms. Sparse vectors keep memory manageable; cosine similarity should define behaviour for zero vectors.'),
      (['contrastive','clip'], 'Form image/text embedding similarities, apply a temperature, and optimise cross-entropy with aligned pairs as positives. Explain symmetric image-to-text and text-to-image losses, in-batch negatives and duplicate positives. Validate target indices and normalisation before measuring retrieval.'),
      (['recommender','recsys','ranking','ctr','feed','cold-start','ad-bidding','ads ranking','search system','app store'], 'Start with the product objective, candidates, labels and serving budget. Separate candidate retrieval from ranking. Address negative sampling, position/selection bias, cold start and feature freshness. Validate offline on temporal splits and online with guardrails; ranking quality, calibration and latency are different concerns.'),
      (['fraud','spam','out-of-distribution'], 'Define the action and cost of false positives versus missed cases. Handle class imbalance, delayed labels, drift and adversarial behaviour. Avoid leaking post-event evidence into training. Calibrate thresholds by capacity and segment, and include human review and monitoring.'),
      (['offline','online','worse online','pipeline'], 'Check training-serving skew, feature freshness, leakage, population shift and mismatched objectives. Compare logged predictions with recomputed features, inspect segments and validate experiment assignment. Reproduce the failure with a minimal example before changing model architecture.'),
      (['parallel','multi-gpu','sharded'], 'Define tensor shapes and partition axes. Data parallelism replicates parameters and reduces gradients; tensor/model parallelism partitions computation; pipeline parallelism partitions stages. Account for all-reduce/all-gather/all-to-all, bandwidth, communication overlap and gradient correctness.'),
      (['research','dissertation','present and defend','resume project','paper','mine novel'], 'Prepare the research question, hypothesis, baseline and contribution. Justify the experiment design, dataset splits and metrics. Explain ablations, uncertainty, failure modes and what result would falsify the claim. Be ready to derive the central method, discuss a limitation and propose a practical next experiment.'),
      (['csv','pandas'], 'Inspect schema, target and missingness before modelling. Split by time or entity as required, build a simple reproducible baseline and fit transformations on training data. Check groupby aggregation and index alignment explicitly. Explain the resulting metric and a useful next experiment.'),
    ]
    for words,text in rules:
        if any(w in t for w in words):return '**Suggested discussion outline**\n\n'+text+'\n\nThis is an original preparation outline, not a quoted company answer or official marking scheme.'
    fallback={
      'ml-system-design':'Clarify the user problem, scale, inputs, labels and latency budget. Sketch data collection, training, serving and monitoring. State a baseline, evaluation metrics and key failure modes. Explain trade-offs and how an online experiment would test the proposal.',
      'research':'State the hypothesis and assumptions. Derive the central result or algorithm, compare a baseline, and propose an ablation that could refute the idea. Discuss reproducibility, uncertainty and limitations.',
      'ml-coding':'Clarify inputs, output shapes, objective and numerical conventions before coding. Implement a small readable version, test edge cases and compare against a simple reference. Discuss computational complexity and vectorisation without changing the result.',
      'stats-probability':'Define the random variables and assumptions. Derive the quantity step by step; distinguish conditional and unconditional probabilities. Use a small simulation as a check, and explain uncertainty and when the assumptions fail.',
    }
    return '**Suggested discussion outline**\n\n'+fallback.get(topic,'Define the concept and its assumptions, give a concrete example, and compare alternatives. Explain how you would check your answer with an experiment or small implementation. The source report provides a short prompt rather than an official answer.')
